# Target C-II — near-floor near contacts force singleton mass or a large rhombic ear-train graph

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proof-only structural refinement; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** the concrete low-detour terminal window from `target_c_free_terminal_t_near_floor_near_contact_matching_2026-09-18.md`  
**Depends on:** the `0.4155D` near-orbit pentagon floor from that note; the exact singleton/full-flower incidence inequality; the no-return-to-one-face train theorem; the train-angle coordinate representation; global contact degree at most five  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_free_terminal_t_near_floor_rhombic_ear_train_graph_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The preceding theorem produces many near-unit pentagon diagonals at the actual new
terminal `t` frontier, but does not ensure that their two boundary sides belong to
rhombic train coordinates. This note supplies that bridge.

In the concrete window

\[
\limsup t_D\le1.959952849540479\ldots,
\qquad
\limsup {b-P_F\over D}\le10^{-5},
\tag{ET.1}
\]

the preceding theorem gives, after the standard limiting extraction,

\[
\lim_{\theta\downarrow0}\liminf
{P_5^{near}(\theta)\over D}
\ge
N_0,
\qquad
N_0=0.415550892775345\ldots .
\tag{ET.2}
\]

Here `P_5^{near}(theta)` consists of bounded pentagons whose five side directions are
within `theta` of one dominant three-line `pi/3` orbit.

The new conclusion is the following split:

\[
\boxed{
{S\over D}\ge0.1-o(1)
}
\tag{ET.3}
\]

for the singleton stopped-train count `S`, or, for every metric tolerance
`eps_geom>0`, there is a family of at least

\[
\boxed{
(0.0239-o(1))D
}
\tag{ET.4}
\]

pairwise vertex-disjoint near-unit ear diagonals, each lying in an empty fully-rhombic
pentagon and each controlled by two distinct rhombic stopped-train angular
coordinates. Moreover one can retain at least

\[
\boxed{
(0.0119-o(1))D
}
\tag{ET.5}

of those ears so that, **before imposing the rhombic hole-period equations and the
external contact/diameter locks**, a single raw train-rate vector strictly decreases
all of their squared lengths simultaneously.

Thus the low-detour near-`t` branch has a more specific remaining obstruction than a
bare near-contact matching: either a linear singleton population survives, or the
period/external locking system must suppress a positive-density directed forest of
locally compatible ear-tightening train inequalities.

---

## 1. From near pentagons to near fully-rhombic pentagons

Let `F_5^{full}` be the number of bounded pentagons with a strict rhombus on the
opposite side of every one of their five contact edges. The exact incidence theorem is

\[
\boxed{
P_5-F_5^{full}\le2S.
}
\tag{ET.6}
\]

Indeed every non-full pentagon has a singleton boundary edge and one singleton edge can
witness at most two pentagons.

Both `P_5^{near}(theta)` and `F_5^{full}` are subsets of the full pentagon population
`P_5`. Hence

\[
\begin{aligned}
|P_5^{near}(\theta)\cap F_5^{full}|
&\ge
P_5^{near}(\theta)+F_5^{full}-P_5\\
&\ge P_5^{near}(\theta)-2S.
\end{aligned}
\tag{ET.7}
\]

If (ET.3) fails, so that `S/D<0.1+o(1)`, then (ET.2) and (ET.7) give

\[
\boxed{
\lim_{\theta\downarrow0}\liminf
{|P_5^{near}(\theta)\cap F_5^{full}|\over D}
\ge
N_0-0.2
=0.215550892775345\ldots .
}
\tag{ET.8}
\]

Every pentagon counted in (ET.8) is source-empty by the existing isolate-host theorem.

---

## 2. Each such pentagon has two train-supported near-unit ears

For sufficiently small `theta`, the five-root closure lemma gives two disjoint adjacent
edge pairs in every near-orbit pentagon. Each pair spans an ear diagonal `d` with

\[
1<d\le2\cos(\pi/3-\theta).
\tag{ET.9}
\]

Because the pentagon is fully rhombic, both sides of each ear are terminal sides of
non-singleton strict-rhombus stopped trains. The two trains supporting one ear are
distinct: otherwise one complete train would have two terminal occurrences on the same
bounded face, contradicting the no-return-to-one-face theorem.

Choose one of the two ears from every pentagon counted in (ET.8). The chosen ear
diagonals are distinct. Their auxiliary graph on contact-core source vertices has
maximum degree at most five, exactly as in the existing near-contact matching proof.
A greedy matching therefore retains at least one ninth of the chosen ears.

Consequently, in the non-singleton branch,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {|\mathcal M_{ear}|\over D}
\ge
{N_0-0.2\over9}
=0.023950099197260\ldots .
}
\tag{ET.10}
\]

For every fixed `eps_geom>0`, choose `theta` small enough that the upper endpoint in
(ET.9) is below `1+eps_geom`. Then all edges of `mathcal M_ear` have lengths in
`(1,1+eps_geom)`.

---

## 3. Exact derivative of one ear in train-angle coordinates

Take one selected ear. Orient its two consecutive unit contact sides along the
pentagon boundary as vectors `u` and `v`, with turning angle

\[
0<\vartheta<\pi.
\]

Let their two stopped trains have angular rates `alpha` and `beta`. The train-angle
cochain gives

\[
\dot u=\alpha Ju,
\qquad
\dot v=\beta Jv.
\tag{ET.11}
\]

The ear vector is `d=u+v`. Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
{d\over dt}|d|^2
&=2(u+v)\cdot(\alpha Ju+\beta Jv)\\
&=2\sin\vartheta\,(\alpha-\beta).
\end{aligned}
\tag{ET.12}
\]

Since `sin(vartheta)>0`, the ear strictly shortens to first order exactly when

\[
\boxed{\alpha<\beta.}
\tag{ET.13}
\]

Thus every selected near-contact ear contributes one strict order inequality between
its two train angular coordinates. No metric approximation is used in the derivative
formula; near-lattice geometry is needed only to make the nonedge itself almost active.

---

## 4. The ear/train incidence graph has maximum degree two

Build a multigraph `J` whose vertices are complete non-singleton stopped trains and
whose edges are the selected vertex-disjoint ears in `mathcal M_ear`; an ear joins its
two supporting trains.

A complete stopped train has exactly two terminal face-side occurrences. At a fixed
terminal pentagon, the selected family uses that train at most once. Therefore

\[
\boxed{\Delta(J)\le2.}
\tag{ET.14}
\]

So every component of `J` is a path or a cycle, allowing a two-edge parallel cycle in
the multigraph convention.

Orient each edge of `J` according to the strict inequality (ET.13) that shortens its
ear. Delete one edge from every underlying cycle. Since every cycle has at least two
edges, at least half of all ear edges remain. The underlying retained graph is a
forest, so its oriented inequalities are acyclic. Assign train rates by any topological
ordering. This produces one raw train-rate vector satisfying every retained strict
ear-shortening inequality simultaneously.

Hence there is a subfamily `M_tight` with

\[
\boxed{
|\mathcal M_{tight}|
\ge{1\over2}|\mathcal M_{ear}|,
}
\tag{ET.15}
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {|\mathcal M_{tight}|\over D}
\ge
{N_0-0.2\over18}
=0.011975049598630\ldots .
}
\tag{ET.16}
\]

A conservative coefficient is `0.0119D`.

---

## 5. What the raw simultaneous tightening does and does not mean

The train-rate vector in Section 4 lives in the unconstrained stopped-train coordinate
space. It is **not** yet asserted to integrate to a contact-framework flex. The audited
train-period theorem imposes two scalar period equations per independent rhombic hole,
and the full active framework adds non-rhombus contact and diameter locks.

The point of (ET.16) is to isolate those equations as the only remaining obstruction to
the local sign pattern. The ear inequalities themselves are globally compatible on a
linear subfamily: there is no directed-cycle obstruction after losing only a factor two.

This gives a concrete next linear-algebra target. Let `K_train` be the kernel of the
rhombic hole-period map. Prove either

1. a positive fraction of the forest inequalities (ET.13) remains strictly feasible on
   `K_train` and can then be combined with the active diameter inequalities; or
2. the period/external row restrictions needed to kill all such directions have rank
   or stress cost linear in `|M_tight|`, contradicting one of the terminal currency
   ledgers.

A pure row-count argument is known to be insufficient in general; the required input
must use the sparse path/cycle support of the ear/train graph or the geometry of the
active diameter rows.

**Disposition:** in the concrete low-detour near-`t` window, either at least `0.1D`
singleton trains survive, or there are at least `0.0239D` vertex-disjoint near-unit
ears supported by rhombic train pairs, with a raw simultaneously tightenable subfamily
of at least `0.0119D`. C-II remains open.
